+# Six-Week Statistics Study Plan
+
+## Assumptions
+
+- Week 1 begins Monday, June 8, 2026, and the six-week window ends July 19. The cumulative exam is Friday, July 17, 2026, from 9:00-11:00 a.m.
+- The recurring schedule is Monday independent study (75 minutes), Tuesday study group from 6:30-7:30 p.m. (60 minutes), Wednesday independent study (75 minutes), and Friday low-stakes quiz plus error review (45 minutes). Sunday stays completely free with no study.
+- Available resources are *OpenIntro Statistics (4th ed.)*, the course problem bank, the class formula sheet, six 20-question practice sets, and the error log.
+- Probability and confidence intervals are the two weakest areas. Their weeks therefore include two retrieval rounds plus targeted error-log work while the original topic sequence, session durations, and group format remain intact.
+- Readiness means scoring at least 80% on each of the final two 20-question cumulative practice sets before the exam.
+
+## Weekly plan
+
+| Week & dates | Focus and measurable outcome | Session plan | Tangible deliverable | Checkpoint |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Week 1 — June 8-14 | Data displays and descriptive statistics; correctly choose a display and calculate and interpret center and spread on at least 16 of 20 mixed items. | **Monday (75 minutes):** Read the relevant *OpenIntro Statistics (4th ed.)* examples, then recreate one histogram and one boxplot from the course problem bank and annotate shape, center, spread, and outliers.<br>**Tuesday group (60 minutes, 6:30-7:30 p.m.):** Compare solutions to eight display/summary problems, with each person explaining two choices aloud using the class formula sheet only to check notation.<br>**Wednesday (75 minutes):** Complete 20 descriptive-statistics items from the course problem bank without notes, score them, and correct every miss in the error log.<br>**Friday (45 minutes):** Take a fresh 10-item low-stakes quiz from the course problem bank, then write in the error log a one-sentence cause and corrected method for each miss. | A one-page annotated data-display guide that embeds the corrected solutions for the 20-item Wednesday set. | At least 16/20 on Wednesday and 8/10 on Friday; every missed item has a complete error-log entry. |
+| Week 2 — June 15-21 | Probability rules and conditional probability; solve at least 17 of 20 mixed probability items and justify when events are independent. | **Monday (75 minutes):** Retrieve the addition, multiplication, and conditional-probability rules from memory, check them against the class formula sheet, and solve 12 course problem bank items.<br>**Tuesday group (60 minutes, 6:30-7:30 p.m.):** Run the first extra retrieval round using 10 oral prompts from the course problem bank, then diagram and teach two conditional-probability problems per person.<br>**Wednesday (75 minutes):** Run the second extra retrieval round with a timed 20-question probability practice set; score it and redo misses without viewing the original work.<br>**Friday (45 minutes):** Take a 10-item probability quiz from the course problem bank and use the error log to sort misses as rule choice, setup, arithmetic, or interpretation. | A probability decision tree covering complements, unions, intersections, independence, and conditional probability, with two worked examples. | At least 17/20 on Wednesday and 8/10 Friday, with no repeated rule-choice error after correction. |
+| Week 3 — June 22-28 | Random variables, normal distributions, sampling distributions, and the central limit theorem; correctly identify the relevant distribution and solve at least 16 of 20 items. | **Monday (75 minutes):** Read the assigned *OpenIntro Statistics (4th ed.)* sections and make six retrieval cards linking parameter, statistic, expected value, and standard error.<br>**Tuesday group (60 minutes, 6:30-7:30 p.m.):** Solve eight normal and sampling-distribution problems from the course problem bank and have each member explain one central-limit-theorem condition.<br>**Wednesday (75 minutes):** Complete 20 mixed items from the course problem bank without notes, then use the class formula sheet to audit notation and formulas before logging corrections.<br>**Friday (45 minutes):** Take a 10-item quiz from the course problem bank, record every miss in the error log, and redo each missed item from a blank page after a 10-minute delay. | A checked six-card retrieval deck linking the key quantities and contrasting population, sample, and sampling distributions. | At least 16/20 Wednesday and 8/10 Friday, including 3/4 correct on central-limit-theorem condition items. |
+| Week 4 — June 29-July 5 | Confidence intervals for means and proportions; select, calculate, and interpret the right interval on at least 17 of 20 items. | **Monday (75 minutes):** Retrieve the interval workflow from memory, verify conditions with the class formula sheet, and complete eight mean/proportion interval problems from the course problem bank.<br>**Tuesday group (60 minutes, 6:30-7:30 p.m.):** Run the first extra confidence interval retrieval round using the course problem bank: teach the full choose-check-calculate-conclude process, then critique six interpretations for parameter, confidence level, and context.<br>**Wednesday (75 minutes):** Run the second extra retrieval round with a timed 20-question confidence interval practice set; score it, correct it, and add condition or interpretation misses to the error log.<br>**Friday (45 minutes):** Take a 10-item mixed interval quiz from the course problem bank and use the error log to rewrite every flawed interpretation in context. | A means-versus-proportions interval checklist containing six corrected interval interpretations. | At least 17/20 Wednesday and 8/10 Friday, with all conditions identified and at least 5/6 interpretations fully correct. |
+| Week 5 — July 6-12 | Hypothesis tests for means and proportions; execute the full testing workflow and earn at least 80% on the first final 20-question cumulative practice set. | **Monday (75 minutes):** Build the null/alternative, conditions, statistic, p-value, and conclusion for eight course problem bank tests, using the class formula sheet only after each setup.<br>**Tuesday group (60 minutes, 6:30-7:30 p.m.):** Compare eight test setups, defend tail direction and conclusion wording, and teach back two patterns from earlier entries in the error log.<br>**Wednesday (75 minutes):** Take the first final 20-question cumulative practice set under timed, closed-note conditions; score it and enter all misses in the error log.<br>**Friday (45 minutes):** Redo the Wednesday practice-set misses cold, record the corrections in the error log, then take a five-item hypothesis-test quiz from the course problem bank focused on the weakest error category. | A corrected first cumulative practice-set packet with a one-page hypothesis-test workflow as its cover sheet. | At least 80% (16/20) on the first final cumulative set and 4/5 on Friday; all misses are corrected with a stated cause. |
+| Week 6 — July 13-19 | Correlation, simple linear regression, and cumulative review; interpret slope, intercept, residual, and association correctly and earn at least 80% on the second final 20-question cumulative practice set. | **Monday (75 minutes):** Complete 12 correlation/regression items from the course problem bank, annotate one output table, and retrieve slope, residual, and correlation interpretations without notes.<br>**Tuesday group (60 minutes, 6:30-7:30 p.m.):** Teach one regression interpretation each, then run a mixed oral review driven by the shared error log, emphasizing probability and confidence intervals.<br>**Wednesday (75 minutes):** Take the second final 20-question cumulative practice set under timed, closed-note conditions; score it, correct every miss, and check recurring formulas against the class formula sheet.<br>**Friday (45 minutes):** Before the 9:00-11:00 a.m. exam, complete a calm 10-item retrieval check drawn from the course problem bank, then review only the class formula sheet and the highest-frequency cues in the error log. | A corrected second cumulative practice-set packet with a single-page formula, conditions, and error-cue sheet attached. | At least 80% (16/20) on the second final cumulative set, with no uncorrected repeat errors; record the July 17 exam as the final performance check. |
+
+## Progress checks
+
+- **Weekly evidence:** Record every Wednesday and Friday score in the error log, label each miss by cause, and verify the week's numeric checkpoint before moving its recurring errors into the next Monday session.
+- **Midpoint check:** At the end of Week 3, confirm that the latest 20-item score is at least 16/20 and that every error-log item has a successful cold redo. If either condition is unmet, use the first 20 minutes of the existing Week 4 Monday and Wednesday sessions for those items without adding a Sunday session.
+- **Weak-area check:** After Weeks 2 and 4, compare the two extra retrieval rounds. Probability must finish at 17/20 or better; confidence intervals must finish at 17/20 or better with at least 5/6 interpretations correct.
+- **Final readiness check:** The first final 20-question cumulative practice set in Week 5 and the second final 20-question cumulative practice set in Week 6 must each reach at least 80% (16/20). If the first misses the target, use the existing Week 6 Monday session for its top two error categories before taking the second.
+- **Exam check:** Sit the cumulative exam on Friday, July 17, 2026, from 9:00-11:00 a.m.; afterward, leave the remaining weekend and Sunday free of study.
